es of Mathematica it does so  here it's taking its morphologically  binarized images after adjusting it it  does component measurements to determine  the centroid and then it does a parallel  map on the center points for each image  it does an image pad on it and that  shifts it to a target position and the  target position I'm picking is the  center of the frame the target X and y  equals image dimensions so the best  frame divided by 2 and then I  so the aligned frames are aligned  planetary images and this is all in in  the notebook that you can you can  download so once I do that I get a  series of image you can see the Jupiter  here has been shifted to the center of  the frame and it's not jumping around as  law a lot as much but you can still see  it's kind of moving around in size and  shape and right now with the techniques  I'm using I can't really get around that  but that's kind of ok because it all  averages out in the end as I said if you  want you can do further alignment using  the alignment functions and if you have  very high quality images you can  you can further do that should divide  this up into more slides so you can use  image align but that that works best if  you have very visible planetary features  okay so after you get the images aligned  then it's actually pretty simple so what  I do is I basically just convert them to  data and just take a mean or a median of  all the images so that basically since  they're all stacked on top of each other  you can imagine the image stack as a  several array a bunch of arrays and each  column a column of pixels you're taking  the the mean or the median of that and  I'm not going to do that right now  because it takes a few minutes and we're  running out of time but this is the  result here and then you need to stretch  it and the reason for that is  astronomical images quite often  concentrate the data into just a small  fraction of the range of values that  could be so here you can see the RGB  channels are all concentrated in the  range 0 to 0.4 so we can easily stretch  this and this will bring up the image so  now you can see a lot more color detail  on the planet and then the final thing  you need to do is you need to do some  sort of image processing to remove the  fuzziness and fuzziness is generally  described by something called the point  spread function and for some images I've  talked about this in earlier talks it's  possible to estimate the point spread  function by just examining the image but  we  can't really do that now we could do  that if there were stars in the image  that were were fairly distinct and then  we picked them out and and then figure  out how they deviated from a point but  we can't do that right now because they  don't have any stars in the image so  we're going to just guess and a good  guess is a Gaussian matrix and you can  see the point spread function kind of  looks like this so this would be a star  in the center which has been blurred out  and see what that looks like a little  more clearly it's something that looks  like this so then we need to assume that  the image is the convolution of this  function with the real image and so we  can use our image deconvolution  functionality to remove this and y r-d  convolution is a spectral deconvolution  method and it tends to be pretty good  because one problem with deconvolution  methods is they tend to introduce  artifacts and so that's our final image  and you can start seeing some some  details cloud structures on the planet  this is not bad considering how bad the  initial images were you can also do more  sophisticated techniques like one  technique which is favored in astronomy  is is something called maximum entropy  deconvolution and I will and this is an  iterative method and this is basically  came out of radio astronomy but it works  good for visible images too I'm not  going to go into this too carefully but  this is based on some some NASA code and  you can look at this later the good  thing about this is it doesn't introduce  too many artifacts and it converges  rather slowly so you can see what you're  getting pretty quick preed generally now  one problem with dealing with large  number of  images is you tend to run out of memory  especially if you're dealing with  thousands and thousands of frames so a  smart way to do this is to process  things in chunks and the Wolfram  language easily lets you do that because  you can take the frame list that we saw  before you can partition it into chunks  of 32 frames and then stack each one of  those chunks and then stack the final  list this takes a little bit longer but  you don't run out of memory and your  computer doesn't crash which is always  an advantage and so here's some quick  examples that's the scope I used to take  all these pictures it's not a big scope  and if I import the stuff going to and  so here's a series of frames this is  just a few of the frames but you can see  one of the artifacts this is not an  artifact on the planet but that's an  artifact on my CCD camera you can see  that stick stain still it's a dust blob  enemy of astrophotographers the world  over and so that if I stack that and  it's going to quickly go through this so  I'm gonna stack it and then do the image  processing and rotation you can see the  final image here which shows a lot of  detail on the planet's surface including  the Great Red Spot and to show you that  we know that that's actually the right  place I asked Wolfram Alpha to compute  the position of the Great Red Spot on  the date that that image was the date  and time that image was taken and  hopefully  get some results here so you can see  it's in this same position and as a  little aside you can also see I actually  took several sequences and and you can  see the planets rotating so Jupiter  rotates once every nine hours so in  between the time I took the first  sequence in the second sequence it had  shifted over a little bit and people  with more patience and time than I have  actually take movies of this and just  showed you do rotating all the way  around so any questions
 

